course: dictionary
lesson: 14356
title: Leaving things out
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To omit something means to leave it out. You can do it on purpose, or by mistake. «Mum omitted the onions from the soup, because Sam hates them.» Mum did not put the onions in. She left them out on purpose.
explain: Omit is a verb, so its forms change: omit, omits, omitted, omitting. «The writer omitted her name from the letter by mistake.» She forgot to write it. Her name was left out, but she did not mean to leave it out.
explain: In school, you may be told not to omit a step in your working. That means every step must be shown. In maths, leaving out a number can change the answer, so check your sum before you finish.
explain: Omit can also be formal, meaning to fail to do something. «He omitted to tell us the time of the match.» Here he did not do the thing he should have done. To check the meaning, swap in «left out» or «forgot». If it still makes sense, you have it.
fact: Omit means to leave something out.
fact: You can omit something on purpose or by mistake.
fact: «Omitted» is the past tense of omit, and «omitting» is the -ing form.
fact: In maths, omitting a number can change the answer.
fact: Skip is a close synonym of omit: both mean leaving something out, though skip often sounds more casual.
exercise: «Mum omitted the onions from the soup.» What does omitted mean? | *Left out | Cooked slowly | Tasted again | Added twice
exercise: Your teacher says, «Do not omit any step in your working.» What should you do? | Write only the answer | *Show every step | Rub out your working | Skip the hard steps
exercise: In maths, what can happen if you omit a number from a sum? | Nothing ever changes | *The answer can change | The numbers get bigger on their own | The sum becomes a poem
test: «He omitted to tell us the time of the match.» What does omitted to tell mean here? | He told us twice | He told us the wrong day on purpose | He told us very loudly | *He failed to tell us
test: Which word is closest in meaning to omit? | shout | *skip | paint
exam: «The writer omitted her name from the letter by mistake.» Why was her name missing? | She wrote it twice | *She left it out by mistake | She had no pen | She wrote it in capitals only
